  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"110\" data-end=\"218\"><strong data-start=\"110\" data-end=\"135\">Sodium in body fluids</strong> (such as pleural, peritoneal, cerebrospinal, or pericardial fluid) is measured to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"220\" data-end=\"526\">\n<li data-start=\"220\" data-end=\"288\">\n<p data-start=\"222\" data-end=\"288\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"231\" data-end=\"260\">fluid-electrolyte balance</strong> in localized compartments</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"365\">\n<p data-start=\"291\" data-end=\"365\">Differentiate between <strong data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"335\">types of effusions</strong> (e.g., transudate vs exudate)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"437\">\n<p data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"437\">Investigate causes of <strong data-start=\"390\" data-end=\"437\">ascites, pleural effusion, or CNS pathology</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"438\" data-end=\"526\">\n<p data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"526\">Aid in diagnosing conditions like <strong data-start=\"474\" data-end=\"489\">peritonitis</strong>, <strong data-start=\"491\" data-end=\"507\">tuberculosis</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"512\" data-end=\"526\">malignancy</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"528\" data-end=\"636\">Sodium levels in fluid are often interpreted alongside serum sodium, osmolality, and other fluid parameters.</p>\n<p data-start=\"528\" data-end=\"636\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dehydration in surrounding tissues</strong></td>\n<td>Can lead to concentrated sodium in the fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High serum sodium (hypernatremia)</strong></td>\n<td>May reflect serum sodium status if barriers between compartments are intact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Iatrogenic causes</strong></td>\n<td>Hypertonic saline infusions or procedures affecting local fluid balance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"528\" data-end=\"636\">&nbsp;</p>",
